    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media across the political spectrum agree that the current asylum and return system is seen as insufficiently effective, prompting repeated calls for concrete mechanisms such as Return Hubs or external deportation centres to accelerate expulsions. Reports from all outlets also converge on the observation that high‑profile incidents involving asylum seekers – alleged crimes or security breaches – generate political fallout, leading to resignations of migration officials and heightened public debate on safety. In addition, coverage uniformly acknowledges that external pressures, for example the situation in Ceuta or the influx of Ukrainian refugees, shape German policy choices and fuel demands for tighter border controls.",
      "controversial_points": "The clash between national sovereignty and EU burden‑sharing defines the policy debate. Conservatives frame migration as security risk, linking asylum to crime and cultural erosion. Left‑leaning outlets emphasize humanitarian duty and integration costs, challenging restrictive return‑hub agreements. Populist media amplify individual scandal stories to fuel anti‑immigrant sentiment, creating moral panic."
